title A lantern that fired four times was read four times and understood once
prompt fleet lap -- open the round, read the rota, take the next agent-doable work reds-first
rota lap 4230, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; the fixed seat is Lindy, and its test is an ear rule: will this line still read true three years on? A ceiling that must rise once per collision, forever, answers no on its own terms -- which is what named the first lap. The dual seat, the crossing, then named the second: a guard that cannot make a sound guards nothing, and one that cannot finish its sentence explains nothing.
think Cold roster read guards_red=4 -- reds_ledger_monotone, reds_row_present, reds_spine_derive, standing_equipment reading roster_broken off the other three.
think Two roots. numbers_double_bound stood at 2 against a ceiling of 1 because %592 became a second published double, and reds_spine_derive died at rishi line 46 with StringTooLong while its own scan read verdict=ok.
think I repaired both, booked row 20260908.001446, folded it, and rebased -- into six conflicts. A peer had landed the same monotone repair at 00:17:03 and another had cured the StringTooLong at the witness. My lap was a duplicate on both halves.
think Their StringTooLong fix is better than mine and I said so rather than arguing for mine. A bare `say scan.out` writes the captured value out without opening a StrBuf at all, so the whole reading prints however large it grows; my bound at ten truncated a diagnosis to fit a buffer. The bound was in the wrong place and their answer removes it rather than shrinking what passes through it.
think So the lap withdrew whole -- parked on branch withdrawn-20260908-001446, adopted the anointed order by reset. Third collision of my night, and the first that cost the tree nothing because both halves already stood upstream.
think Then the question their fix left open, which nobody had asked: how many other sites make the promise that one had just broken? 502 `say` lines interpolate a captured .out or .err, and 1,741 assert-else clauses do.
think The four firings are the argument. reds_spine_derive tonight, readme_metrics_splice on an 18 KB file, tame_style_long_fn and its scan twice more -- four careful hands, four correct local repairs, four lessons written into four different files' comments, and no sentence anywhere saying this is one class.
obs Two shapes, counted apart because they fail at different moments. Eager composes on every run and reds a GREEN tree the day its scan grows. Deferred composes only in an assert's else clause, which is to say exactly when an explanation is owed -- the worse half, and the peer's own sharpest sentence about their own fix.
obs Ratchets rather than gates. The backlog was not made today, and every site's repair is the same two-line rewrite that loses nothing, so no reader is asked to give up a diagnosis to satisfy the guard. safe is reported beside the hazards so the answer arrives with the count.
obs And the ceilings are SHARES rather than counts, which I learned by watching my own first draft refuse. I set raw ceilings at exactly the measured 502 and 1,741; the rebase closing this same round brought two peers' new guards in, deferred read 1,744, and the guard reddened over work nobody did wrong. Writing a witness is the most ordinary act on this pier. A count of a growing population measures the fleet's size; what these readings are about is a habit -- of the sites that could reach for the safe shape, how many do -- and a per-mille share says that where a count cannot. Proven by a control leg rather than argued: a pen quadrupled without changing how it writes reads the same 500, and only a pen that writes worse moves it.
obs The slack is named and small -- two and three per mille above the measured 153 and 534, which is room for the two or three sites an ordinary lap adds and none at all for twenty.
obs The width is read off Rishi's own declaration rather than carried as a copy, so a widened buffer moves the reading instead of leaving a stale number in a comment nobody rereads.
obs The guard keeps its own rule -- it prints its reading through a bare say and contributes zero to both of its own counts. A guard asking for a habit it does not keep is teaching one thing and doing another.
obs An error of my own, caught by reading the output rather than trusting it: the width first read 40968, because the regex swept the 8 of u8 into the digits.
obs A second, caught by the control: the scan's root walk climbs from its own $0, so called at its real path from a pen it walked back into this tree and read 502 sites inside a pen holding three files. Each pen carries its own copy of the scan now.
obs A third, in my own hands and squarely in the lane the card already opened: I read the process table twice with a bare pattern and twice got a peer's tree back -- once a ps -e showing Copal editing an ITINERARY, once a pgrep matching Petrichor's --hot --scoped, which had me waiting on a run that was never mine. Both times readlink /proc/PID/cwd settled it.
obs And a fourth worth booking by someone: the roster's own directory lock records a BARE PID. A stale lock here named 2158552, my pass refused against it by name, and that pid was a live process in grain-petrichor. The reaper asks kill -0, which cannot tell a foreign or recycled pid from its owner. Named rather than taken -- the repair is to record the tree beside the pid, or read the pid's cwd before believing it.
obs A fifth, and it is the same lesson as the third wearing a different coat: standing_equipment's live_leader_parent_reads_gone leg reds when the pass runs under setsid, which my own elder card block had already recorded and I had already forgotten. Three hot passes died with the shell that launched them and a fourth reddened on the launcher rather than on the tree. The pass that ships was run from an ordinary shell.
obs Two peers' faults met on the way out and were repaired with the tools that exist for them: a depth-lost link in my own newly shelved elder block, and a misordered pair of rows on the shared day shelf, %440's class, sorted by index_shelf_repair.sh.
obs I also broke the card's hold-still directive: I edited REDS.md's headline while a hot pass ran, and the runner answered tree_moved=yes and withheld its receipt, exactly as designed. The pass that ships was run over a still tree.
